1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,769
Dispatch settings decide more than
which button the dispatcher presses.

2
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:08,189
They affect the intended audience,
automatic activity changes, and the

3
00:00:08,189 --> 00:00:10,579
information needed before creating a call.

4
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:14,729
Open Department Settings, then
Call and Dispatch Settings.

5
00:00:15,170 --> 00:00:18,709
Begin with a written example: who
should receive a search assignment,

6
00:00:18,969 --> 00:00:22,780
what should the unit status become,
and what information must accompany it?

7
00:00:23,299 --> 00:00:26,870
Our fictional Firehouse fifty-one
roster provides the screen data.

8
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:31,670
The same decisions apply to a mobile
mental health team, a security patrol, a

9
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:34,479
wildland crew, or a plant emergency team.

10
00:00:34,939 --> 00:00:38,349
A software dispatch is an
assignment; it does not establish

11
00:00:38,349 --> 00:00:42,860
qualifications, clinical scope,
arrival, or a completed response.

12
00:00:43,259 --> 00:00:47,790
In this isolated training department, real
outbound notifications are suppressed.

13
00:00:48,169 --> 00:00:52,750
We can inspect saved configuration and
resource transitions, but this exercise

14
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:55,110
does not prove external alert delivery.

15
00:00:55,309 --> 00:01:00,089
Use Shift for Group Dispatch resolves the
on-duty roster when a group is selected.

16
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:04,359
Read the fallback displayed on
this screen: an empty shift result

17
00:01:04,359 --> 00:01:05,939
falls back to group members.

18
00:01:06,219 --> 00:01:10,530
Do not assume that switching it on
guarantees an on-duty-only audience.

19
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:14,999
Check a populated shift and an empty
shift before relying on the setting.

20
00:01:15,519 --> 00:01:19,570
We enable shift dispatch and Set
Status for Shift Personnel on

21
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:24,329
Dispatch, selecting Responding for
dispatch and Standing By for release.

22
00:01:24,710 --> 00:01:27,910
These activity changes are separate
from staffing availability.

23
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:32,300
For mobile response, check the
actual scheduled team; for search and

24
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:34,689
rescue, check the mobilization roster.

25
00:01:35,079 --> 00:01:38,640
Security and industrial departments
should review site or facility

26
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:40,239
coverage and shift boundaries.

27
00:01:40,669 --> 00:01:44,349
Wildland crews need to consider
overnight and extended assignments.

28
00:01:44,770 --> 00:01:48,730
This recording verifies the saved
choices; it does not run every

29
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:50,520
roster and fallback combination.

30
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:55,259
Group-Scoped Dispatch limits supported
call lists, maps and nearest-resource

31
00:01:55,259 --> 00:01:59,490
views to the member’s group subtree,
with exceptions for reported or assigned

32
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:04,209
calls, department administrators and
explicitly selected department-wide roles.

33
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:09,470
The screen explains the scope; review
real actors before changing access.

34
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:12,849
We leave scoping off and
add no role exceptions.

35
00:02:13,289 --> 00:02:16,990
A security company can review site
visibility while its central desk

36
00:02:16,990 --> 00:02:21,060
needs a broader view; an industrial
department can review facility groups.

37
00:02:21,609 --> 00:02:26,319
Under Unit Dispatch Settings, Also
Dispatch to Assigned Personnel expands

38
00:02:26,319 --> 00:02:28,350
an assignment to the unit’s current crew.

39
00:02:28,859 --> 00:02:32,990
Also Dispatch to Entire Group can
reach a much larger station audience.

40
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:37,359
We enable assigned-crew expansion
and leave whole-group expansion off.

41
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:41,940
An empty or stale crew assignment
remains a problem; a saved switch

42
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:44,120
cannot establish who is actually aboard.

43
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:49,039
This exercise does not claim that crew
recipients or real alerts were verified.

44
00:02:49,250 --> 00:02:52,499
The department-wide unit
defaults use built-in meanings.

45
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:57,519
Our defaults are Responding on
dispatch and Released on release.

46
00:02:57,929 --> 00:03:02,269
The unit type override table appears
for types with custom unit statuses.

47
00:03:02,639 --> 00:03:06,499
For the Rescue type, we choose
Responding to assignment on dispatch

48
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:08,939
and Ready for assignment on release.

49
00:03:09,329 --> 00:03:13,390
Both options belong to the Field Response
set created in the earlier lesson.

50
00:03:13,759 --> 00:03:16,810
We added Responding to assignment
with the Responding meaning

51
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:18,230
before selecting it here.

52
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:22,389
Searching assigned area remains a
later declaration by the field team.

53
00:03:22,799 --> 00:03:26,389
Automatically assigning Searching
at call creation would imply that

54
00:03:26,389 --> 00:03:28,299
search activity had already begun.

55
00:03:28,659 --> 00:03:32,729
Use the same distinction for a patrol
sent to an investigation, a mobile

56
00:03:32,729 --> 00:03:37,330
team traveling to a visit, a wildland
crew responding to staging, or an

57
00:03:37,330 --> 00:03:39,269
industrial team approaching an incident.

58
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:42,950
Consider whether automatic
readiness on release fits local

59
00:03:42,950 --> 00:03:46,190
procedures when equipment checks
or crew recovery are needed.

60
00:03:46,349 --> 00:03:49,820
New Call Form Fields separates
Visible from Required.

61
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:54,460
Core name, nature, priority
and type remain part of intake.

62
00:03:54,859 --> 00:03:58,389
We first save G P S Coordinates
and Note as required.

63
00:03:58,799 --> 00:04:02,820
Creating the synthetic search exercise
without a location is rejected

64
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:04,529
with a geolocation requirement.

65
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:07,869
We then enter both decimal
coordinates and try again.

66
00:04:08,289 --> 00:04:10,750
In this build the same rejection remains.

67
00:04:11,139 --> 00:04:15,350
That is a recorded web-form limitation,
not a successful validation.

68
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:21,139
We return to settings, keep G P S visible
but optional, retain Note as required,

69
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:24,680
and save the working configuration
for the rest of the demonstration.

70
00:04:25,099 --> 00:04:28,549
The note is deliberately filled
in on the successful call; this

71
00:04:28,549 --> 00:04:32,340
lesson does not claim that it proves
empty rich-text note enforcement.

72
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:35,880
Test the web form and each
client your department uses.

73
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:40,470
For remote searches, patrol sites
and plant areas, review coordinates,

74
00:04:40,570 --> 00:04:44,610
addresses and indoor locations against
the actual operating procedure.

75
00:04:44,799 --> 00:04:48,339
Automatic resource selection
can be manual, station based

76
00:04:48,469 --> 00:04:50,230
or closest-resource based.

77
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:54,390
Matching run cards and automatic
dispatch are covered in the next lesson.

78
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:58,560
Here we leave recommendations
on Off, automatic dispatch

79
00:04:58,610 --> 00:05:02,789
unchecked, move-up recommendations
off and the staffing gate off.

80
00:05:03,119 --> 00:05:06,769
The screen warns that units without
defined roles pass the staffing

81
00:05:06,769 --> 00:05:11,099
gate; review unit definitions before
treating a threshold as protection.

82
00:05:11,519 --> 00:05:15,869
A location-age limit rejects old positions
where the selection path applies.

83
00:05:16,160 --> 00:05:18,999
Including stale locations
changes that assumption.

84
00:05:19,429 --> 00:05:22,600
Routed driving estimates use
the configured mapping provider

85
00:05:22,639 --> 00:05:24,460
and can add cost and latency.

86
00:05:24,769 --> 00:05:28,429
Straight-line estimates do not
account for road access, terrain

87
00:05:28,479 --> 00:05:30,379
or a plant’s controlled entrances.

88
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:35,710
Check-in timers need target, duration,
warning and escalation procedures.

89
00:05:35,990 --> 00:05:37,879
We leave automatic timers off.

90
00:05:38,229 --> 00:05:41,909
Timer defaults and call-type
overrides have their own save actions,

91
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:43,800
separate from the main settings form.

92
00:05:44,059 --> 00:05:48,809
Click the main Save for Call and Dispatch
Settings and inspect its success message.

93
00:05:49,219 --> 00:05:51,309
Open Calls, then New Call.

94
00:05:51,690 --> 00:05:57,880
Enter Training, S A R dispatch policy
exercise, choose Rescue and Routine, and

95
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:00,629
use the identifier DEMO SETTINGS zero one.

96
00:06:01,110 --> 00:06:04,809
Enter a clear synthetic nature,
an accountability note and

97
00:06:04,809 --> 00:06:05,920
the training coordinates.

98
00:06:06,290 --> 00:06:09,920
Select Squad Three, which currently
shows Ready for assignment.

99
00:06:10,219 --> 00:06:12,920
Create and Dispatch Call
returns to the dashboard.

100
00:06:13,329 --> 00:06:17,000
The new call is present, and Squad
Three now shows Responding to

101
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:18,840
assignment with a new timestamp.

102
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,279
Open the call and inspect
its saved nature, note,

103
00:06:22,490 --> 00:06:24,369
identifier and Dispatched tab.

104
00:06:24,810 --> 00:06:26,479
Squad Three is recorded there.

105
00:06:26,930 --> 00:06:29,069
These are observed application results.

106
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:32,310
The displayed assignment does
not prove that a device received

107
00:06:32,310 --> 00:06:36,200
a push, that a crew acknowledged
it, or that the unit has arrived.

108
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:38,799
Those need separate operational tests.

109
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:40,730
Close only this training call.

110
00:06:41,179 --> 00:06:44,899
Enter a completion note describing
the configuration test and leave

111
00:06:44,899 --> 00:06:46,809
Send Notification unchecked.

112
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:51,829
After saving, the active list no
longer includes the exercise, and Squad

113
00:06:51,829 --> 00:06:53,880
Three displays Ready for assignment.

114
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:56,709
That verifies the custom
release transition.

115
00:06:57,259 --> 00:07:00,920
For community paramedicine and
mental health response, review team

116
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:05,209
audiences, approved operational
notes and post-visit availability.

117
00:07:05,630 --> 00:07:09,730
For search and rescue, review
mobilization fallback, accountability

118
00:07:09,769 --> 00:07:11,100
and sustained searches.

119
00:07:11,479 --> 00:07:15,049
For security, review patrol
audiences and site coverage.

120
00:07:15,409 --> 00:07:19,039
For wildland contractors,
review crew dispatch, overnight

121
00:07:19,039 --> 00:07:20,920
assignments and rest assumptions.

122
00:07:21,260 --> 00:07:26,130
For industrial response, review facility
audiences and equipment readiness before

123
00:07:26,159 --> 00:07:28,030
automatically releasing a resource.

124
00:07:28,470 --> 00:07:32,370
Your practice exercise is to explain
the intended audience, configure

125
00:07:32,370 --> 00:07:37,490
one transition, create and close a
synthetic call, inspect both changes

126
00:07:37,609 --> 00:07:41,889
and record any client or delivery
behavior still awaiting verification.
